argin toward Lean R, while a red-wave year could push it toward Safe R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Tennessee remains a deeply Republican state in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly fourteen points toward the GOP. No incumbent president is on the ballot, and no published polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available for this race, so the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisan balance. Recent cycles have consistently produced double-digit Republican margins here, and nothing in the current picture points to a departure from that pattern.\n\nThe lack of state polling adds uncertainty, and the national political environment could move the margin in either direction. Even so, the structural Republican advantage is large enough that a Democratic win would likely require an unusually strong national wave. The race is rated Likely Republican, with the GOP favored by about fourteen points.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0603, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1012, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.11, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1055, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0961, "margin": -14.0}]}